// ============================================================
// QUERY: Suspicious Inbox Rule Creation Alert
// Incident: INC-2026-87241
// Table: OfficeActivity
// Purpose: Detect creation of Exchange inbox rules that archive,
//          delete, or hide emails — a common attacker technique
//          for suppressing incident response communications and
//          hiding BEC activity from the compromised user.
// Platform: Microsoft Sentinel
// ============================================================

OfficeActivity
| where Operation == "New-InboxRule"
| where Item contains "Archive" or Item contains "Delete"
| where TimeGenerated >= ago(24h)
| project TimeGenerated, UserId, Item

// ============================================================
// INCIDENT CONTEXT:
//   - Compromised User:   m.smith@lognpacific.org
//
//   RULE 1 — "Invoice Processing" (Created 22:28:22 UTC):
//     Trigger:  FROM j.reynolds@lognpacific.org (CFO)
//     Action:   MoveToFolder → Archive
//     Stop:     StopProcessingRules = True
//     Purpose:  Auto-hide CFO's responses to the BEC email.
//               The CFO's warnings/corrections are invisible to m.smith.
//
//   RULE 2 — "Backup Copy" (Created 22:32:31 UTC):
//     Details:  Partial capture — additional hiding mechanism
//
//   CRITICAL DETAIL:
//     Rule 1 was created BEFORE the BEC email was sent (23:13 UTC).
//     The attacker prepared the cover-up 45 minutes before the attack.
//     This demonstrates premeditated operational security planning.
//
// DETECTION LOGIC NOTES:
//   - The Item field in OfficeActivity is JSON — use parse_json() to extract:
//       extend ItemData = parse_json(Item)
//       | project RuleName = tostring(ItemData.Name),
//                 Conditions = tostring(ItemData.Conditions),
//                 Actions = tostring(ItemData.Actions)
//
//   - High-value alert tuning: ANY inbox rule that targets a specific named
//     sender (especially an executive/manager) AND moves to Archive is
//     extremely suspicious — this almost never has a legitimate purpose.
//
// TUNING:
//   - Add alert for rules where trigger = a specific named sender
//   - Alert on StopProcessingRules = True in rule actions
//   - Consider alerting on ANY inbox rule creation for high-privilege users
//   - Correlate with recent sign-ins from external/anonymous IPs for same user
// ============================================================
